---
import { Badge, Callout, CodeTabs, Icon, Link } from '@santi020k/lumen-astro'

import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'

const disableOsvExamples = [
  { value: 'vscode', label: 'VS Code', language: 'json', code: `{
  "depBeacon.checkVulnerabilities": false
}` },
  { value: 'zed', label: 'Zed', language: 'json', code: `{
  "lsp": {
    "dep-beacon": {
      "settings": {
        "checkVulnerabilities": false
      }
    }
  }
}` }
]
---

<Docs title="Security Signals" description="How Dep Beacon uses OSV.dev vulnerability data and how to control security queries.">
  <header class="docs-page-header">
    <p class="docs-eyebrow"><span>05</span> / Security signals</p>
    <h1>See the risk.<br />Understand the context.</h1>
    <p class="docs-intro">
      An available update and a reported vulnerability are different signals. Dep Beacon keeps that distinction
        close to the dependency.
    </p>
    <dl class="docs-facts">
      <div><dt>Advisory source</dt><dd>OSV.dev</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Queries</dt><dd>Enabled by default</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="severity">
    <h2 id="severity">Read severity, not just color</h2>
    <p>
      Dep Beacon queries OSV for the package and version selected by the declared range, then summarizes advisory
      details in your editor. Inspect the dependency hover for context.
    </p>
    <dl class="docs-status-list">
      <div>
        <dt><Badge class="docs-risk-warning" variant="warning">Low / medium</Badge></dt>
        <dd>Orange warning signals identify lower-severity advisories that still need review.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><Badge variant="destructive">High / critical</Badge></dt>
        <dd>Red error signals give higher-severity findings greater prominence.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><Badge variant="outline">Unknown severity</Badge></dt>
        <dd>Advisory details remain visible as known risk, even when a severity is unavailable.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <Callout class="docs-note">
      <strong>Keep your full audit workflow.</strong>
      <p>These signals describe the version selected from the manifest range. They do not replace an audit of your
        installed dependency tree or a review of the advisory.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="privacy">
    <h2 id="privacy">Control what goes to OSV</h2>
    <p>
      OSV checks send package names and selected versions to OSV.dev. Set <code>checkVulnerabilities</code> to
      <code>false</code> when your workspace should not send that information to this external service.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs
      ariaLabel="Disable OSV queries by editor"
      items={disableOsvExamples}
      storageKey="dep-beacon-docs-editor"
      wrap={false}
    />
    <p>
      This disables OSV queries. Dependency metadata requests still go to your configured npm registry;
      it does not make Dep Beacon an offline tool.
    </p>
  </section>

  <nav class="docs-next" aria-label="Related guide">
    <Link href="/docs/configuration">Configure your workspace <Icon name="arrow-right" /></Link>
  </nav>
</Docs>
